23:30:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: I understand the concern—it’s natural for a team to feel cautious when a process introduces something unfamiliar, especially when it involves people who have been incarcerated. The key is to address the nervousness with honesty, empathy, and facts, while also framing the decision in a way that aligns with your warehouse’s values and practical needs. Here’s a step-by-step approach you could take: ### 1. Acknowledge the feelings without judgment Start a conversation by validating their worries. You might say: > “I know the idea of hiring through a prison release programme has made some of us uneasy, and I want to address that openly. It’s okay to have questions—let’s talk through them together.” This creates space for people to voice specific fears (safety, reliability, team dynamics) rather than suppressing them. ### 2. Share accurate information about the programme Much of the anxiety often comes from the unknown. Explain: - **Who the candidates are**: Typically, these are individuals nearing the end of their sentence who have demonstrated good behavior and completed vocational training. They’re not hardened, unpredictable criminals but people preparing to reintegrate into society. - **The screening process**: Most structured re-entry programmes (like those run by government agencies or reputable non-profits) involve thorough vetting, risk assessments, and ongoing support services. You could say: > “The programme doesn’t just send us anyone. They screen participants carefully and provide case managers to support the individuals and us as an employer.” - **The lower recidivism rates**: Studies consistently show that stable employment dramatically reduces the chance of re-offending. When people have a job and a community, they’re far less likely to return to crime. ### 3. Be clear about your own safety and performance standards Make it known that you aren’t lowering your expectations. Outline: - The same background checks and interview process will apply (within legal limits). - The same code of conduct, safety rules, and performance metrics will be used for all employees. - There will be a clear probationary period and a zero-tolerance policy for any workplace misconduct. This reassures the team that you’re not sacrificing safety or accountability—you’re simply widening the talent pool thoughtfully. ### 4. Invite a representative from the programme to talk If possible, arrange a Q&A session with someone from the re-entry organisation. They can answer technical questions, share success stories, and explain the support structure. Sometimes hearing directly from a professional eases fears more than anything you say as a manager. ### 5. Highlight the positive impact on the team and culture Gently shift the conversation to the bigger picture: > “Many of us believe in second chances, and this is a tangible way we can make a difference. It can also build a culture of empathy and reliability—people who’ve been given a fair chance often become some of the most loyal, hardworking team members.” If you can find it, share a concrete example from another warehouse or similar business that has done this successfully. ### 6. Set up safeguards that make everyone comfortable Consider practical steps like: - Pairing new hires with experienced buddies for the first few weeks. - Having a clear point of contact (HR or a manager) to address any concerns immediately. - Running a voluntary workshop on unconscious bias or “fair chance hiring” so the team feels equipped rather than anxious. ### 7. Keep the door open for ongoing feedback End with something like: > “We’ll take this one step at a time, and I want you to come to me if anything feels off. We’ll review how it’s going after the first month and adjust if needed. This is our decision as a team, and your comfort matters.” By blending empathy, information, and clear boundaries, you can help your team move from nervousness to cautious optimism—and possibly even pride in being part of something meaningful.
